00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:56.850
Like, I bought a used one off eBay.

00:23:56.850 --> 00:24:01.050
So that, it's interesting this,
my psychology along the way.

00:24:01.050 --> 00:24:03.750
It's good for me to notice
this in myself because I don't,

00:24:03.750 --> 00:24:05.433
it's something to overcome.

00:24:06.433 --> 00:24:09.870
If someone's trying something
creative kind of like me,

00:24:09.870 --> 00:24:13.230
but like, I would rationalize things like,

00:24:13.230 --> 00:24:18.230
I'm not gonna buy a deck new
because then I'll feel bad

00:24:18.450 --> 00:24:20.310
when I don't follow through with this.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:23.820
So I'm gonna buy a used one
off eBay so I won't feel

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:26.640
as bad if I walk away from this.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:29.760
It's such an, you know, it was
like I was giving myself outs

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:32.717
in full instead of like
saying, you know what?

00:24:32.717 --> 00:24:35.190
I think I could be really into this.

00:24:35.190 --> 00:24:36.813
I'm going to go for it.

00:24:39.617 --> 00:24:40.609
At so many.

00:24:40.609 --> 00:24:41.442
Buy something so new and expensive

00:24:41.442 --> 00:24:42.810
that you'll feel guilty if you stop.

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:44.210
That's what I would've done.

00:24:45.170 --> 00:24:46.003
You know, you're like.

00:24:46.003 --> 00:24:47.455
Right.

00:24:47.455 --> 00:24:48.690
Like guilt yourself into not stopping.

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:49.523
Right.

00:24:49.523 --> 00:24:50.580
I kind of had this predisposition,

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.230
oh this probably won't turn into anything.

00:24:52.230 --> 00:24:54.720
I'm gonna encounter something
that's gonna be the reason

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:57.990
that like, I just never
follow through with it,

00:24:57.990 --> 00:25:00.270
and that's why I like when I,

00:25:00.270 --> 00:25:01.453
even when I was doing Mythicon,

00:25:01.453 --> 00:25:03.330
I was like, I'm not gonna
make a big deal out of this.

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:06.300
You know, it's not a healthy instinct.

00:25:06.300 --> 00:25:08.550
I think it's something
that I've had to overcome,

00:25:08.550 --> 00:25:10.710
and that's why I just, I'm going back

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:13.547
trying to acknowledge it
'cause I'm learning from it,

00:25:13.547 --> 00:25:18.547
but I just really started
getting into, I love music,

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:20.130
I love listening to music.

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:22.890
Like especially over the
pandemic, I got really into it.

00:25:22.890 --> 00:25:26.792
So then, like being able to
manipulate music and turn knobs,

00:25:26.792 --> 00:25:31.350
there's like a, the editor
in me and I'm talking like,

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:33.360
video editor in me that
I don't do anymore,

00:25:33.360 --> 00:25:38.340
it's like that really came
to, that came back to me

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:40.470
like using the deck.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.823
It's just a lot of fun.

00:25:41.823 --> 00:25:42.656
And you like making playlists

00:25:42.656 --> 00:25:45.650
for certain, to set vibes.
I love making playlists.

00:25:45.650 --> 00:25:48.987
Yeah, yeah, and greatest
compliment you can give me is,

00:25:48.987 --> 00:25:53.835
can I have the playlist you were playing?

00:25:53.835 --> 00:25:58.835
So as I started getting ready
for Mythicon, I was like,

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:02.130
I kept, I just didn't, there's two things.

00:26:02.130 --> 00:26:04.230
It's like working on what
songs you're gonna play

00:26:04.230 --> 00:26:07.080
and then working on the skills
of a DJ to be able to like,

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:09.720
operate the deck and
transition between songs

00:26:09.720 --> 00:26:12.810
and put things in an order,
kind of make it seamless,

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:14.610
and once you start watching tutorials

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:17.220
and reading about stuff,
it gets overwhelming

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:22.220
really quickly, and at that
point, Ben here at work,

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:26.160
our, you know, DP,
director on a lot of stuff,

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:28.260
he was like, you know, I gotta tell you,

00:26:28.260 --> 00:26:31.410
I got a DJ deck recently too.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.300
I was like, really?

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.634
I was like, dude, we should
get together and like,

00:26:35.634 --> 00:26:39.174
do some DJ stuff.
Some double DJ.

00:26:39.174 --> 00:26:43.830
So it was like three weeks
before Mythicon that we finally

00:26:43.830 --> 00:26:47.910
were able to get together
and Ben is, you know,

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:52.910
very technical and so when,
he had all these things

00:26:53.040 --> 00:26:54.872
to show me that I had not figured out,

00:26:54.872 --> 00:26:57.990
and so over the course of
a few hours of him like,

00:26:57.990 --> 00:27:02.169
showing me some transitions
and he had his own deck

00:27:02.169 --> 00:27:03.960
and he would show me stuff
and then he would try

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.870
to figure out how to do it on his deck,

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:07.500
on my deck.
How's his deck

00:27:07.500 --> 00:27:08.850
compared to yours, he got a bigger deck?

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:12.000
His deck is not as good as mine.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:12.960
Oh, okay.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:14.790
But he probably paid more
'cause I got mine used.

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:16.910
Okay, well interesting.

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:21.077
So he was teaching me
things I didn't know,

00:27:21.077 --> 00:27:24.164
which was pretty much
everything, and I just,

00:27:24.164 --> 00:27:27.386
I'll tell you and I
don't know how much Ben,

00:27:27.386 --> 00:27:29.730
I don't know what his
perspective on it was,

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:33.268
but like when we parted ways that night,

00:27:33.268 --> 00:27:37.620
I stayed there for another
three hours freaking out

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:40.163
and trying to practice 'cause I was like,

00:27:40.163 --> 00:27:42.810
oh my God, I didn't realize
how much I didn't know

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:46.530
and now I'm gonna suck so bad.

00:27:46.530 --> 00:27:49.149
Well, had you been like,
before Ben showed up,

00:27:49.149 --> 00:27:52.710
were you like putting together a playlist

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:54.570
and like, practicing
transitions and stuff?

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:57.084
Like, what was like,
'cause I know so little

00:27:57.084 --> 00:28:00.666
about how this works from
a technical perspective.

00:28:00.666 --> 00:28:02.820
I don't know anything about it.

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:06.270
I had to, you gotta pick a bunch of songs

00:28:06.270 --> 00:28:10.650
and from the reading I
did, it's like however long

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.870
your set is, you need to have three times

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:13.703
that amount of songs.

00:28:13.703 --> 00:28:18.330
So I'm like, I need to be ready
to do two hours at Mythicon.

00:28:18.330 --> 00:28:20.850
Now that reduced some,
but two, so at the time

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:23.360
I was thinking I need six hours of music

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.590
if I'm transitioning.

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:26.670
So explain that to people.

00:28:26.670 --> 00:28:30.510
Because when you play, when
you're reading the room,

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:33.120
you have to be able to go with
the flow of what's happening.

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:35.460
If you're not connecting with an audience,

00:28:35.460 --> 00:28:36.660
you've got to change it up.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:38.340
You gotta be able to go
in a different direction,

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:39.900
like a different style.

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:43.800
You have to like, and I have
kind of an eclectic style,

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:45.270
like I like some pop stuff.

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:48.150
Like, I'm a fan of the Harry Styles album.

00:28:48.150 --> 00:28:52.320
I like to play some Dua Lipa, but then

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:53.880
I'm a real hip hop guy.

00:28:53.880 --> 00:28:56.310
So I really like that, and
I like old school hip hop

00:28:56.310 --> 00:28:58.830
and I like some, a lot of new hip hop,

00:28:58.830 --> 00:29:00.930
but if people aren't into
that and they don't know

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:03.750
the new stuff, then I've
only got the old stuff

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:07.377
and I can't keep going back,
and within any one song,

00:29:07.377 --> 00:29:10.384
if it gets a little
boring, you kinda wanna

00:29:10.384 --> 00:29:13.861
transition to a new song.

00:29:13.861 --> 00:29:17.573
I mean, if a song's five
minutes or four minutes,

00:29:17.573 --> 00:29:20.496
in that, in a certain, in a dance setting.

00:29:20.496 --> 00:29:23.805
Yeah, I don't think you
want, sometimes you just

00:29:23.805 --> 00:29:26.460
don't wanna play more
than like half of it.

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:27.706
Yeah.

00:29:27.706 --> 00:29:29.220
You know, you wanna keep things moving.

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:30.780
Like, the moment they
start to get a little,

00:29:30.780 --> 00:29:34.470
okay, I'm, everybody's
not loving this song,

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:36.870
you gotta move on and then
you gotta figure out a way

00:29:36.870 --> 00:29:41.580
to do it that is as seamless as possible.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:42.420
Right.

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.130
You know, so you can move
from one thing to the next.

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:45.690
So I understand all these principles.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:50.690
Like, I know how to read a
room, I have my taste in music.

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:53.700
I only wanna play things
that I like that then

00:29:53.700 --> 00:29:55.650
also connect with people.

00:29:55.650 --> 00:30:00.450
So I had all these exercises
in my mind that was like,

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:04.560
song selection matters so
much, and then technique

00:30:04.560 --> 00:30:09.560
like beat matching where
you have a song and then

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:13.710
you queue up another
song in your earphones

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:15.780
and you match the beat.

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:20.250
So like you move the tempo
up or down to be in sync

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:22.590
with the other song
and then you find a way

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:25.260
to start playing it and
mixing the two together

00:30:25.260 --> 00:30:29.340
and then subtly transitioning
in a way that seems like

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:30.690
you've done sort of a remix.

00:30:30.690 --> 00:30:33.990
And that's why DJ has like
headphones that are like

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:35.580
one ear on, one ear off.

00:30:35.580 --> 00:30:37.050
You're listening to what
the crowd's hearing,

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:39.480
but then you're listening what
you're queuing up and playing

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.970
because like, you don't
want this long intro.

00:30:41.970 --> 00:30:44.274
Oh yeah, this is an album
cut and like, the artist

00:30:44.274 --> 00:30:47.033
is talking or doing a skit or something.

00:30:47.033 --> 00:30:49.560
This is like, you're
gonna kill the room if,

00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:52.110
you gotta start it at the right time.

00:30:52.110 --> 00:30:54.090
So all of these, and there's so much more

00:30:54.090 --> 00:30:57.390
that I still don't know,
but in terms of being able

00:30:57.390 --> 00:31:00.870
to transition, I mean, and then
you can do like key matching

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:02.400
'cause you don't wanna be blending stuff

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.020
that are in different keys.

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:06.240
It'll be like dissonant and it doesn't,

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:07.290
it just is like, ick.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:10.530
There's so much technique that
goes into it that I'm just

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:13.554
scratching the surface,
but I made up my mind

00:31:13.554 --> 00:31:15.720
in the limited amount of time I had,

00:31:15.720 --> 00:31:18.600
I was like, I cannot,
and it was that night

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:21.240
that I kind of decided, I
cannot put this much pressure

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:25.740
on myself to like, be a
technically proficient DJ.

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:30.120
I just, I'm not there yet
if, but I can't lose sight

00:31:30.120 --> 00:31:33.588
of some key realities.

00:31:33.588 --> 00:31:38.588
The first is there is, this is
gonna be the most supportive,

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:42.510
enthusiastic crowd to just,
and a lot of them will know

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:45.176
the story that I'm telling
like, that where this,

00:31:45.176 --> 00:31:46.713
that I'm just getting started with this.

00:31:46.713 --> 00:31:47.736
Right.

00:31:47.736 --> 00:31:50.340
And all they want to
do is just watch me try

00:31:50.340 --> 00:31:53.760
this new endeavor and
support me through it.

00:31:53.760 --> 00:31:58.760
It was, there probably won't
be a gathering to watch,

00:31:59.022 --> 00:32:01.837
I'll just skip ahead and tell you.

00:32:01.837 --> 00:32:03.715
Based on my experience, there was not,

00:32:03.715 --> 00:32:07.860
I don't think there is a
DJ whose first public set

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:12.450
was in such a supportive,
enthusiastic environment ever.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:13.444
Yeah.

00:32:13.444 --> 00:32:14.490
You, it may be the most.

00:32:14.490 --> 00:32:15.660
It was the.

00:32:15.660 --> 00:32:16.500
Supportive debut.

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:18.450
The most awesome.

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:19.283
Yeah.

00:32:19.283 --> 00:32:23.370
Crowd for a DJ who was
just getting started.

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:24.203
It was amazing.

00:32:24.203 --> 00:32:25.579
I was right about that.
I was actually watching

00:32:25.579 --> 00:32:29.719
the DJ who started the night.

00:32:29.719 --> 00:32:30.552
Yeah.

00:32:30.552 --> 00:32:31.620
He's good.
And I was like,

00:32:31.620 --> 00:32:36.303
I was like trying to
get his read on things.

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:38.490
I actually, he was playing it
really close with the vest.

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:41.700
I couldn't get much of
a read, but I could.

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:42.660
On what?

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:45.117
On his read of the crowd or
on the crowd's read of him?

00:32:45.117 --> 00:32:46.890
No, on the fact that
like, 'cause he was kind

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:49.320
of down there in the back kind
of hanging out in the back.

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:50.793
Oh, while I was up there.

00:32:50.793 --> 00:32:51.780
While you were up there and I'm like,

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:54.027
here's this guy that's
been playing for the crowd

00:32:54.027 --> 00:32:56.100
and the crowd is really into it already.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:32:56.933
Right.

00:32:56.933 --> 00:32:58.840
I mean I'm sure he had been
educated on like, you know.

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:00.300
I was really self-conscious.

00:33:00.300 --> 00:33:01.560
He was coming up to do this.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.350
Yeah, there was a DJ who
played and warmed up the crowd

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:07.470
because when our stage show ended,

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:11.447
everybody made their way
to the tent and the DJ

00:33:11.447 --> 00:33:14.670
started playing, the dance
party started and then after

00:33:14.670 --> 00:33:19.503
like 45 minutes is when
I came on and took over.

00:33:20.643 --> 00:33:21.659
And well, and.

00:33:21.659 --> 00:33:23.910
I like literally, I walked
on and then he walked off.

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:24.840
And specifically.

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:27.167
And then he watched and I
was really self-conscious.

00:33:27.167 --> 00:33:28.410
Well let's get into the details of like,

00:33:28.410 --> 00:33:31.440
how you actually started the
set, like what that involved,

00:33:31.440 --> 00:33:33.870
because that was what was a trip to A,

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:35.610
to be there to witness.

00:33:35.610 --> 00:33:36.443
So yeah.

00:33:36.443 --> 00:33:38.490
But also to then like,
turn and watch this guy

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:39.892
'cause like you didn't have a.

00:33:39.892 --> 00:33:41.088
This is what I want to know.

00:33:41.088 --> 00:33:44.490
It wasn't a typical
introduction on any level.

00:33:44.490 --> 00:33:45.569
Nope,

00:33:45.569 --> 00:33:48.919
but the, just to finish my thought though,

00:33:48.919 --> 00:33:52.605
the second point was I,
they're gonna be supportive

00:33:52.605 --> 00:33:53.610
was the first point.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:55.500
Second point that I wanted
to keep in the forefront

00:33:55.500 --> 00:33:58.530
of my mind is that like, I'm
not gonna focus on technique.

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:01.110
I'm just gonna focus on song selection.

00:34:01.110 --> 00:34:05.550
If at the end of the day I
play a song all the way through

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:08.522
and then I play the next
song all the way through,

00:34:08.522 --> 00:34:10.995
I can just do that and it will be okay.

00:34:10.995 --> 00:34:14.277
It, song selection is
so much more important

00:34:14.277 --> 00:34:19.277
than any technique at this
point in the game for me.

00:34:19.291 --> 00:34:24.291
So I spent a lot of time
like amassing playlist,

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:29.682
private playlists that like
got me to six hours of music

00:34:29.682 --> 00:34:34.260
and then I just started working
on putting that playlist

00:34:34.260 --> 00:34:36.870
together in an order and saying, you know,

00:34:36.870 --> 00:34:40.260
you set cue points so if you
need to skip forward in a song,

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:43.230
you can skip forward on
beat and it won't feel like

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:46.980
I'm just guessing at when I
transition to the next song.

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:50.280
If you can skip to the kind
of the outro of the song

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:52.426
and then transition, it
becomes a little easier

00:34:52.426 --> 00:34:57.426
and then, but so that was my focus,

00:34:57.569 --> 00:35:00.450
and then once I got my set list down,

00:35:00.450 --> 00:35:03.630
then I did start just
trying to get proficient

00:35:03.630 --> 00:35:05.490
at going from song to song.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:06.840
It sounds simple, but I was just like,

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:10.305
I just have to keep it simple,
but then I also wanted,

00:35:10.305 --> 00:35:15.305
I was like, well I have
to reveal my DJ name.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:19.260
The first time anyone's gonna hear it

00:35:19.260 --> 00:35:23.430
is gonna be from stage and well, I'm like,

00:35:23.430 --> 00:35:28.430
see DJs, I know they can talk,
but like in the purest form,

00:35:30.960 --> 00:35:35.690
in my opinion, a DJ doesn't
talk but I need to talk

00:35:35.690 --> 00:35:39.335
'cause I need to say what my DJ name is.

00:35:39.335 --> 00:35:41.490
So I said, you know what I'm gonna do?

00:35:41.490 --> 00:35:46.490
I'm going to record little sounds.

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:53.570
I'm gonna record little moments
that announce my DJ name.

00:35:54.128 --> 00:35:59.128
So, you know, I actually got
Mark over and he recorded me,

00:36:04.500 --> 00:36:07.050
and then we like put some
effects on some stuff that like,

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:09.284
then I could load it on my
deck and like hit buttons

00:36:09.284 --> 00:36:14.284
and start playing stuff
that would, you know,

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:18.078
reveal my DJ name, so like this.

00:36:18.078 --> 00:36:22.830
Elkhound .

00:36:22.830 --> 00:36:24.522
That's my voice.

00:36:24.522 --> 00:36:26.640
Oh, I remember hearing that.

